{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Horizon-dependent bias: forecasts are slightly high in the near term (≈days 1–15), but become systematically too low in the late horizon (≈days 20–30) when a sustained uptrend occurs (missed upside tail). Apply an upward tilt that increases with horizon when recent momentum is positive.",
    "Turning-point/mean-reversion error: the model tends to predict a dip/reversion around the mid-horizon (≈days 12–20) that is too deep/too early versus realized prices. Damp the magnitude of forecasted pullbacks unless confirmed by recent consecutive declines.",
    "Underreaction to breakout regimes: after prices break above the prior range (e.g., moving from high-60s into 70s), the model anchors to the old level and underestimates subsequent acceleration. Add a regime-switch uplift when price closes above recent 20–30d highs.",
    "Overshoot on short-lived strength: when the model projects sharp rises (notably late-horizon in some runs), realized outcomes rise but less than projected and can fade afterward. Cap daily slope/acceleration and enforce partial mean reversion after large forecasted jumps.",
    "Volatility mis-timing: forecast volatility is front-loaded (too much day-to-day movement early) while realized volatility clusters later during trend extension. Smooth early-horizon path and reallocate variance to later horizons when momentum/trend signals are strong."
  ]
}
